  "body": {
    "id": "thm:weighted-erasure-spread",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "There exist universal constants \\(c_0,c_1,c_2\\in(0,1)\\), for example \\(c_0=c_2=2^{-12}\\) and \\(c_1=2^{-30}\\), such that the following holds.  For every \\(0<\\alpha\\le1/4\\) and every \\((w,q)\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_{l=1}^Lq_ls_l^2=1\\) and \\(\\|s\\|_\\infty\\sqrt{x}\\le c_0\\),\n\\[\n [-c_2\\sqrt{x},c_2\\sqrt{x}]\\subseteq\\mathcal T_{\\mathrm{tilt}},\n\\]\nand every measurable interval \\(\\Lambda(O,R)\\), including an externally randomized one, obeys\n\\[\n \\inf_{|t|\\le c_2\\sqrt{x}}Q_t\\{T(V)\\in\\Lambda(O,R)\\}\\ge1-\\alpha\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n \\sup_{|t|\\le c_2\\sqrt{x}}\\mathbb E_{Q_t}\\operatorname{len}\\{\\Lambda(O,R)\\}\n \\ge c_1\\min\\{1,\\sqrt{Sx}\\}.\n\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "w",
      "L",
      "q",
      "s",
      "alpha",
      "x",
      "mathcalTtilt",
      "Lambda",
      "O",
      "V",
      "R",
      "Q",
      "t",
      "S",
      "T"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:tilted-sign-prior",
      "def:weighted-erasure-law",
      "lem:weighted-erasure-confidence"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The normalization is exactly \\(\\sum_lq_ls_l^2=S^{-1}\\sum_lw_l^2/q_l=1\\).  The dominance condition says \\(\\|s\\|_\\infty\\sqrt{x}=M\\sqrt{x/S}\\le c_0\\).  Thus, for \\(|t|\\le c_2\\sqrt x\\),\n\\[\n |t|\\|s\\|_\\infty\\le c_0c_2<1,\n\\]\nwhich proves \\([-c_2\\sqrt x,c_2\\sqrt x]\\subseteq\\mathcal T_{\\mathrm{tilt}}\\) and makes every coordinate probability in \\(\\pi_t\\) valid.  The first part of \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}weighted\\text{-}erasure\\text{-}confidence}\\) now gives\n\\[\n \\sup_{|t|\\le c_2\\sqrt x}\\mathbb E_{Q_t}\\operatorname{len}\\{\\Lambda(O,R)\\}\n \\ge c_1\\sqrt{Sx}.\n\\]\nFinally, \\(S=\\sum_lw_l(w_l/q_l)\\le M\\sum_lw_l=M\\).  Combining this with \\(M\\sqrt x\\le c_0\\sqrt S\\) yields \\(\\sqrt{Sx}\\le c_0<1\\).  Hence \\(\\sqrt{Sx}=\\min\\{1,\\sqrt{Sx}\\}\\), proving precisely the requested implication.",
